{
  "title": "Count and Say",
  "description": "The *count-and-say* sequence is a sequence of digit strings. The first term is `\"1\"`. Each later term is made by reading the previous term aloud: go through it from left to right, and for each run of equal digits say how many there are and then the digit.\n\nFor example, `\"1\"` is read as \"one 1\", which gives `\"11\"`. Then `\"11\"` is read as \"two 1s\", which gives `\"21\"`.\n\nGiven `n`, return the `n`-th term of the sequence.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= n <= 30`\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "string count_and_say(int n) {\n    string term = \"1\";\n    for (int step = 1; step < n; ++step) {\n        string next;\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < term.size();) {\n            size_t j = i;\n            while (j < term.size() && term[j] == term[i]) {\n                ++j;\n            }\n            next += to_string(j - i);\n            next += term[i];\n            i = j;\n        }\n        term = next;\n    }\n    return term;\n}\n",
  "template": "string count_and_say(int n) {\n    // Return the n-th term of the count-and-say sequence.\n    return \"\";\n}\n",
  "tags": [
    "Strings",
    "Simulation"
  ],
